  "hypothesis": "De-blobbed Stage-2 and Stage-3 LightGBM auto-context classifiers trained with whole-component hide-draw labels on four input families -- (1) out-of-fold Stage-(k-1) probabilities sampled at +-1..15 px along and across local structure-tensor + 2 m LiDAR scarp strike, (2) multi-scale anisotropic smooths (L in {3,6,12,15} px, W in {1,1.5,2,3} px) and 1D curvilinear ridge-vs-blob contrast (dropping the sigma=10 px isotropic smooth per IR-68-BLOB-01), (3) raw gradient/tilt/Hessian-ridge plus 2 m USGS 3DEP LiDAR antislope scarp & GeoDAWN 1.5 km high-pass radiometric/magnetic channels, and (4) masked-catalogue context (clamped distance, strike alignment, 1 km/3 km spacing ratio, and along-strike tip-continuation drop) -- beat the per-pixel Stage-1 baseline by >2 SEs on outer blocks across all withheld pixels and on pixels >1 km from any mapped fault.",
  "mechanism": "Normal-fault networks in the Great Basin exhibit multi-kilometer along-strike continuity, sharp across-strike profile convexity, antislope 2 m LiDAR scarps, shallow magnetic dipole steps, and potassium/uranium fault-core alteration corridors. Removing 30-100 km regional background levels and adding 1D ridge-vs-blob contrast allows Stage 2 and Stage 3 to link fragmented per-pixel detections across alluvial cover without creating 2D regional blobs.",
  "named_non_fault_process_that_could_mimic_it": "Pleistocene Lake Lahontan / Bonneville shoreline beach ridges, fluvial terrace risers, highway/canal embankments, and tilted volcanic cooling-unit cliffs produce linear 2 m LiDAR steps and local topographic gradients that can mimic fault scarps when aligned with regional structure.",
  "non_fault_mimic": "Pleistocene Lake Lahontan / Bonneville shoreline beach ridges, fluvial terrace risers, highway/canal embankments, and tilted volcanic cooling-unit cliffs produce linear 2 m LiDAR steps and local topographic gradients that can mimic fault scarps when aligned with regional structure.",
